With a sport- and human rights-based approach, this project will equip adolescent girls and young women (AGYW) at risk or survivors of violence in 3 rural districts in Zambia to: realise their right to safety, equality and respect; challenge existing socio-cultural norms that perpetuate violence and poor health outcomes; and serve as gender equality advocates. We propose a girl-centered package to address factors at multiple levels that put AGYW at risk of adverse sexual and reproductive health (SRHR) outcomes, prevent equitable participation in sport and communities, and limit access to life saving health and social services, stipulating:
 - IF AGYW build health and life skills assets, including gender equitable norms and comprehensive SRHR knowledge and the confidence to use it;
 -IF girls, their male peers and communities have access to SRHR information and services along with youth-friendly referral pathways;
 -IF AGYW have opportunities to lead sustainable sport-based peer support networks and mobilise human rights-based advocacy; 
 -IF AGYW are empowered to engage with key stakeholders in community, government and the health system to improve GBV response mechanisms and inform actualisation of national policies in their communities;
 -THEN AGYW will be empowered to equitably and fully participate in society, access services to meet their SRHR needs and experience improved health and life outcomes as they transition to adulthood, including reduced incidence of GBV, HIV, early pregnancy, and child marriage.

 We will build on momentum achieved through project Phase 1 while prioritising AGYW involvement in defining their own challenges, desired changes and solutions to shape Phase 2. With the addition of two community-based partners, Muchinga Corridors (MC) and Adolescent Reproductive Health Advocates (ARHA), Grassroot Soccer Zambia (GRS) and partners will collaborate to build capacity in the successful sport for change and human right-based model at the regional level while enhancing project sustainability and geographic reach via a localised approach.

COVID19 -In light of the current COVID-19 pandemic, the Positive Change Partnership (PCP) is working to find innovative solutions to mitigate the severe impact that the crisis is having on black and minoritised victim-survivors of sexual violence who are also some of the most vulnerable service users. Since the pandemic hit, the 5 black led VAWG organisations who make up the partnership have seen a 30-50% increase in demand for our services, particularly from women or services working with women who have no recourse to public funds or women who face multiple barriers. In line with public health messaging the PCP has had to close our public centres (which were community based hubs) and halt face-to-face support, and extend its online and telephone services, which are already experiencing an extra demand. The partnership has also seen a high increase in demand from referrals and requests from statutory services in relation to migrant women?s needs. Although the referrals are presented as domestic violence- 90% of the women referred have experienced sexual violence. Victim-survivors are presenting at a higher level of risk, often enduring many forms of abuse before reporting, and only doing so because the levels of harm have escalated or they feel that their life is being threatened. Women are facing higher levels of sexual exploitation and violence (online, in families/communities and from intimate partners). After steering group discussions about the impact of Covid, the level of need in relation to support needs and the increase in high risk referrals, we predict a scaled-up need for services that remains at the current lockdown level of a 30-50% increase. Unlike mainstream generic and commissioned services, the PCP has had to self-resource and increase services, and have not received any funding support from their local authorities. In order for the partnership to work effectively and in order to reach a wide range of victims/survivors, key resources need to be purchased and provided to service users as well as to better resource our frontline project workers whose caseloads have increased. Funding is urgently needed across the 5 specialist organisations.

 The Positive Partnership requires:

 - Additional financial support to be able to provide service users with access to technology, including smart phones, iPad?s, tablets and pay as you go phones that could be a lifeline for the women we support.60% of our service users do not have credit on their phones in order to make phones calls to contact their VAWG advocate should they require support if they were feeling low, required safety advice or wanted support to plan a move away from their perpetrator. 25% of our service users do not have access to their own independent phone and therefore do not have the means to call the emergency services in the scenario that they are at risk of serious harm/homicide.;
 - We require financial support to purchase essential resources for the travelling foodbank we have in place for our most vulnerable service users;
 - We require financial support to upgrade the digital platforms that all the services use so that we can expand to a live chat function in relation to live queries that require an instantaneous response. We would like to establish digital live chat platforms so service users (not only VAWG victims/survivors) can access instant support through different forms of communication if they would prefer instant messaging over phone calls. This would also be for black and minority women survivors who may be feeling isolated/lonely and would benefit from additional emotional support. 
 - As a result of the 30- 50% increase in referrals into the service, we require financial support to be able to increase the current hours of some staff to support in the capacity of meeting these new demands and the 30% increase in time that it takes to support each woman as a result of remote working and multi-lingual clients;
 - We need to increase our emotional wellbeing services for the sexual violence survivors we support - additional hours of counselling, group therapy and emotional wellbeing support;
 - We require financial support to provide PPE for frontline staff and first aid kits to our destitute service users who struggle to buy this themselves. This would include supplies that would support service users who develop covid-19 related symptoms. PCP will then be able to provide the most at risk and destitute domestic and sexual violence victims with essential protection and safety;- women with vulnerabilities and underlying health conditions will not need to leave their homes, their nutrition intake, cleanliness and immunity will be improved and their mental health, wellbeing and anxiety will be improved as they will without worry, have access to essential items.

Change the Narrative & Evidence the Prevalence and Impact of the No Recourse to Public Funds Rule on Migrant BME Women Facing Gender Based Violence in the UK 

 ?it?s all just too crippling, I have no recourse to funds... it?s all still emotionally daunting. The government says ?alone, together? but some of us, including, myself, feel that society has forgotten us. The government has forgotten vulnerable women.? 
 Survivor Testimony, House of Lords, June 2020 
 Southall Black Sisters (SBS) has been supporting and campaigning for the rights of BME women and children for over 4 decades, our work has always been focused on the rights of migrant women and children, particularly those with no recourse to public funds (NRPF). We have been at the forefront of the struggle to end violence against women and girls (VAWG) in the UK, raising public and social awareness around women?s rights whilst ensuring that we amplify the voices of survivors.

 We urgently need support to challenge and end the widespread violation of NRPF women?s rights which puts women at risk of violence, abuse and death. The Change Makers grant would address this by:

 ? Qualifying the Evidence: Production of reflective national NRPF data and the BME women?s sector?s first Social Value/Economic Gains Model in 3 areas; Advocacy, Mental Health and Accommodation;
 ? Shifting the Narrative: Amplification of the voice of NRPF survivors through the centralized inclusion of Women?s Voices and ?storywork methodology? to inform campaigning, policy and models of change;
 ? Strengthening Networks: Improved national networks from diverse community engagement through to local authority partnerships and commissioners via regionalized networks led by the national partnership; 
 ? Sustaining Solutions: Building capacity and resources, activating policy change, embedded knowledge networks and socio-political/cultural shifts to positively influence legislation and higher level strategic decision making locally, regionally and nationally.
